==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|acroamatick||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|en|la|acroamaticus}}, {{m|la|acroaticus||esoteric}}, at first chiefly in reference to the “esoteric” and originally oral teachings of {{w|Aristotle}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἀκροαματικός||for hearing only; esoteric}}, from {{m|grc|ἀκροάομαι||to listen}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|rare|chiefly|archaic}} Of or pertaining to hearing.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1632|author=Thomas Randolph|page=59|location=Cambridge|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/jealousloverscom00rand/page/58/mode/2up|title=The Jealous Lovers|text=Prate they not cataracts of insensible noise, / That with obstreperous cadence cracks the organs / '''Acroamatick''', till the deaf auditor / Admires the words he heares not?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|title=Critique of Pure Reason|tlr=F. Max Müller|page=590|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/0330Bk/page/n671/mode/2up|by=Immanuel Kant|original=Kritik der reinen Vernunft|text=I should prefer, therefore, to call the former '''''acroamatic''''', or audible (discursive) ''proofs'', because they can be carried out by words only (the object in thought), rather than ''demonstrations'', which, as the very term implies, depend on the intuition of the object.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|editors=Hans Adler; Wulf Köpke|publisher=Camden House|isbn=9781571133953|page=129|title=A Companion to the Works of Johann Gottfried Herder|chapter=Herder and Language|author=Jürgen Trabant|text=The idea that the first thought, as an internal event, is always also dialogical, that is, possesses a communicative quality in itself, is connected to its '''acroamatic''' origin.}}
# [[esoteric|Esoteric]], [[abstruse]]; {{lb|en|in particular}} taught [[orally]] to select [[student]]s and not [[disseminate]]d.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|author=w:Laurence Lampert|title=Nietzsche and Modern Times: A Study of Bacon, Descartes, and Nietzsche|isbn=0300065108|page=68|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=V159dk4GjWcC&pg=PA68|text=How to ''begin'' such warfare against sovereign religion in times ruled by the very zealots against whom one must fight? How else but covertly, enigmatically, in the '''acroamatic''' manner practiced so beautifully in ''Holy War''?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author={{w|Philip Culbertson|Philip L. Culbertson}}|title=A Word Fitly Spoken: Context, Transmission, and Adoption of the Parables of Jesus|isbn=0791423115|page=38|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=RO2_nOOfdcsC&pg=PA38|text=Repeatedly in the Gospels Jesus takes his disciples aside to offer them '''acroamatic''' teachings not intended for the mass of listeners or to explain a secret meaning to some parable with which he has entertained the larger crowd.}}
# {{senseid|en|education}} {{lb|en|education}} Based on [[lecture]]s or [[exposition]] by [[monologue]].
#: {{coord|en|erotematic}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1959|author=Josef Andreas Jungmann|title=Handing on the Faith: A Manual of Catechetics|page=28|text=Up to that time the “'''acroamatic'''” method had been used in which the catechist gave a lecture, and the children were only listeners ({{l|grc|ἀκροᾶσθαι|tr=-}}).}}
#* '''2015''', Nils F. Schott, [https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=Mle1CgAAQBAJ&pg=PA108 "A Mother to All" in ''Love and Forgiveness for a More Just World'', p. 108, n. 45]:
#*: [Questions’] employment here does not mark a shift from the '''acroamatic''' (lecture-based) to the erotematic (interrogatory) method, for the answers are not known.

